.\" Man page for btrfs-backup-ng restore
.TH BTRFS-BACKUP-NG-RESTORE 1 "January 2026" "btrfs-backup-ng" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
btrfs-backup-ng-restore \- restore snapshots from backup location
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fISOURCE\fR \fIDESTINATION\fR
.br
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore
\fB\-\-list\fR \fISOURCE\fR
.br
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore
\fB\-\-volume\fR \fIPATH\fR \fB\-\-to\fR \fIDESTINATION\fR
.br
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore
\fB\-\-list-volumes\fR
.br
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore
\fB\-\-status\fR \fISOURCE\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Pull snapshots from backup storage back to a local system. This command
enables disaster recovery, migration between machines, and point-in-time
restoration.
.PP
The restore command automatically handles incremental chains, ensuring all
parent snapshots required for a successful restore are transferred.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I SOURCE
Backup location to restore from. Can be a local path (e.g., /mnt/backup/home)
or an SSH URL (e.g., ssh://user@host:/path/to/backup).
.TP
.I DESTINATION
Local path to restore snapshots to. Must be on a btrfs filesystem. Created if
it does not exist: it is an output location, the place this command was asked
to put something new, unlike a backup location, which must exist (see
\fBPATHS\fR in \fBbtrfs-backup-ng\fR(1)).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Listing and Selection
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List available snapshots at backup location without restoring.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-snapshot " " \fINAME\fR
Restore a specific snapshot by name.
.TP
.BI \-\-before " DATETIME"
Restore the snapshot closest to the specified time. Format: YYYY-MM-DD or
YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Restore all snapshots from the backup location (full mirror).
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Interactively select which snapshot to restore from a list.
.TP
.BI \-\-fs-checks " auto|strict|skip"
Filesystem verification mode: 'auto' (warn and continue), 'strict' (error
on failure), 'skip' (no checks). Default: auto.
.TP
.B \-\-skip-remote-lock
Proceed even if a pin cannot be recorded on the location, wherever its lock
store lives (a remote target's lock directory, or a local location's lock
file). Only safe when nothing else can prune this location during the run. A
medium mounted read-only needs no pin and none is asked for.
.TP
.B \-\-skip-verify
Skip the pre-restore integrity check for raw backups (restore even if the
stored stream's checksum no longer matches, and skip the extra read). Use
for last-copy recovery of a partially-corrupt backup.
.TP
.BI \-\-timestamp-format " FMT"
strftime format for parsing snapshot timestamps in direct mode (defaults
to the config's [global] timestamp_format, else the built-in).
.SS Transfer Options
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Print the restore plan and stop. It is the same plan a run prints and
executes, and it refuses what the run would refuse.
.TP
.B \-\-no-incremental
Send every snapshot in full (no \fB\-p\fR). The chain a snapshot depends on
is still brought, as full sends.
.TP
.BI \-\-compress " METHOD"
Compress the transfer from an \fBssh://\fR backup location: the remote runs
\fBbtrfs send\fR through the compressor and this host decompresses before
\fBbtrfs receive\fR. Any method \fBrun \-\-compress\fR accepts (zstd, gzip,
lz4, xz, ...). Other sources ignore it: a local source has no wire, and a
\fBraw://\fR or \fBraw+ssh://\fR stream is sent exactly as it was stored.
The decompressor must be installed on this host; the restore refuses before
connecting when it is not.
.TP
.BI \-\-rate-limit " RATE"
Bandwidth limit for transfers. Examples: 10M, 1G, 500K.
.TP
.B \-\-progress
Show progress bars during transfers.
.TP
.B \-\-no-progress
Disable progress bars.
.SS Safety Options
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
Not supported in this release. Snapshots already at the destination are
left exactly as they are and a warning says so; anything missing is still
restored. To replace an existing snapshot, remove it yourself and run the
restore again. Why the option does not replace, and what a replacement
must satisfy, is in \fIdocs/RESTORE-OVERWRITE.md\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-in-place
Not implemented in this release. The command refuses and restores nothing
(exit status 2). A restore receives each snapshot as a nested subvolume at
\fIDESTINATION\fR/\fIname\fR and never replaces \fIDESTINATION\fR itself.
To replace a location, restore into a staging directory, verify it, and
swap the subvolumes yourself; the README's Strategy 2 gives the commands
and the rollback. The running root filesystem can only be replaced from a
rescue system.
.TP
.B \-\-yes-i-know-what-i-am-doing
Confirm dangerous operations. It does not unlock \fB\-\-in-place\fR.
.SS SSH Options
.TP
.B \-\-ssh-sudo
Use sudo for btrfs commands on the remote host.
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh-key " FILE"
Path to SSH private key file.
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh-auth-sock " PATH"
Explicit ssh-agent socket (overrides auto-discovery; useful under sudo).
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh-host-key-policy " POLICY"
Host-key verification for this run: \fBaccept-new\fR (default) or \fBstrict\fR.
.SS Decryption Options
.TP
.BI \-\-gpg-keyring " PATH"
GPG keyring to decrypt an encrypted raw backup (\fBraw://\fR / \fBraw+ssh://\fR).
Must match the keyring the backup was encrypted for.
.TP
.BI \-\-openssl-cipher " CIPHER"
OpenSSL cipher fallback for a legacy raw backup whose \fB.meta\fR sidecar does
not record one. Modern sidecars record the cipher and are authoritative, so
this is only consulted for backups written before that was sealed.
.SS Other Options
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix " PREFIX"
Filter snapshots by prefix (e.g., "home-" to only show home snapshots).
.TP
.B \-\-no-fs-checks
Skip btrfs subvolume verification. Needed when backup directories are
regular directories rather than btrfs subvolumes.
.SS Config-Driven Restore Options
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Path to configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-\-volume " PATH"
Restore backups for volume defined in config (e.g., /home). Uses the
backup target(s) configured for that volume.
.TP
.BI \-\-target " INDEX"
Target index to restore from (0-based). Default: first target (0).
Use with \fB\-\-volume\fR to select which backup target to restore from.
.TP
.BI \-\-to " PATH"
Destination path for config-driven restore. Required when using \fB\-\-volume\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-list-volumes
List volumes and their backup targets from the configuration file.
.SS Recovery Options
.TP
.B \-\-status
Show status of locks and incomplete restores at the backup location. A
location holding snapper backups (numbered
.B .snapshots/<n>
slots, or raw streams with
.B .snapper-meta.json
sidecars) lists those backups as well, each with the pins a snapper restore
holds on it.
.TP
.BI \-\-unlock " " [\fILOCK_ID\fR]
Unlock stuck restore sessions. Use "all" to unlock all restore locks,
or specify a specific lock ID.
.TP
.B \-\-cleanup
Remove what an interrupted restore left at \fIDESTINATION\fR. Each receive
is recorded in a run marker under
\fIDESTINATION\fR/.btrfs-backup-ng/restore/ and the marker is removed once the
copy is verified. A subvolume is deleted only when a marker whose restore is no
longer running names it, directly under the destination (never \fB..\fR,
never through a symlink), and it has no received_uuid. Everything else is
reported and left, including any subvolume no marker names, however empty.
Honours \fB\-\-dry-run\fR and asks for confirmation.
.SH RESTORE STRATEGIES
.SS Strategy 1: Direct Restore (Default)
Restore the latest snapshot directly:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrfs-backup-ng restore /mnt/backup/home /mnt/restore \\
    --prefix "home-" --no-fs-checks
.fi
.RE
.SS Strategy 2: Specific Snapshot
Restore a specific point in time:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrfs-backup-ng restore /mnt/backup/home /mnt/restore \\
    --snapshot home-20260101-120000 --no-fs-checks
.fi
.RE
.SS Strategy 3: Interactive Selection
Choose from a list:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrfs-backup-ng restore /mnt/backup/home /mnt/restore \\
    --interactive --no-fs-checks
.fi
.RE
.SS Strategy 4: Full Mirror
Restore all snapshots:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrfs-backup-ng restore /mnt/backup/home /mnt/restore \\
    --all --no-fs-checks
.fi
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
List available snapshots:
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore --list /mnt/backup/home --no-fs-checks
.TP
Restore latest snapshot from local backup:
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore /mnt/backup/home /mnt/restore --no-fs-checks
.TP
Restore from SSH backup with sudo:
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore ssh://backup@server:/backups/home /mnt/restore --ssh-sudo
.TP
Restore specific snapshot:
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore /mnt/backup/home /mnt/restore --snapshot home-20260101-120000
.TP
Restore snapshot closest to a date:
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore /mnt/backup/home /mnt/restore --before "2026-01-01 12:00"
.TP
Check for stuck locks:
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore --status /mnt/backup/home --no-fs-checks
.TP
Unlock all stuck restore sessions:
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore --unlock all /mnt/backup/home --no-fs-checks
.TP
Clean up partial restores:
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore --cleanup /mnt/restore
.TP
List configured volumes and targets:
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore --list-volumes
.TP
List snapshots for a configured volume:
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore --volume /home --list
.TP
Restore using config (knows backup location):
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore --volume /home --to /mnt/restore
.TP
Restore from second configured target:
.B btrfs-backup-ng restore --volume /home --target 1 --to /mnt/restore
.SH INCREMENTAL CHAINS
A restore brings the chain a snapshot depends on: the older snapshots at a
btrfs backup location, or the parent recorded in the \fB.meta\fR sidecar of a
\fBraw://\fR store. Whatever is already at the destination is skipped, and
"already there" is decided by identity (the copy's received_uuid against the
backup's own), never by name. Each snapshot is sent as an increment from the
newest one the destination holds, or in full when it holds none. A request that
is already satisfied succeeds with "Already at the destination, so nothing to
do".
.PP
Example chain:
.PP
.RS
.nf
snap-1 (full) -> snap-2 (incr) -> snap-3 (incr) -> snap-4 (incr)
.fi
.RE
.PP
To restore snap-4 onto an empty destination, snapshots 1, 2, 3 and 4 are all
transferred; if snap-2 is already there, only 3 and 4 are.
.SH WHAT IS ALREADY AT THE DESTINATION
Before anything is transferred, every name the restore would receive is
examined. When the destination holds something under that name that is not
this backup's copy (a partial from an interrupted restore, a subvolume made by
hand, a copy of a different snapshot, a plain directory), the whole restore
refuses, names the path and what it is, and transfers nothing. It is never
deleted. A \fBraw://\fR increment whose parent is no longer in the store is
refused the same way, since a stored increment applies only onto its parent.
.SH RECOVERY FROM FAILURES
A transfer that fails is cleaned up by the restore itself: the partial copy it
made is removed and its pin on the backup is released. A restore that is killed
outright (SIGKILL, power loss) leaves a partial subvolume and its run marker,
and the next restore refuses to receive onto it. A pin a killed restore held
on an \fBssh://\fR or \fBraw+ssh://\fR target expires on its own a few minutes
later. To recover at once:
.PP
.IP 1. 3
Check status: \fBbtrfs-backup-ng restore --status SOURCE\fR
.IP 2. 3
Unlock any stuck sessions: \fBbtrfs-backup-ng restore --unlock all SOURCE\fR
.IP 3. 3
Clean up partial restores: \fBbtrfs-backup-ng restore --cleanup DESTINATION\fR
.IP 4. 3
Retry the restore
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR btrfs-backup-ng (1),
.BR btrfs-receive (8),
.BR btrfs-send (8)
